<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=abo-blood-group-system">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">ABO blood group system</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ABO blood types</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ABO blood group system classifies human blood by the presence of the A and B antigens on red blood cells; the Austrian physician Karl Landsteiner discovered it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a>, dividing blood into three groups in 1901 after finding that some people's serum clumped others' red cells. His students found a fourth group, later named AB, the following year. It is the most important blood group system for transfusion, since a mismatch can cause a potentially fatal reaction. Landsteiner won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> for it in 1930.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abo-blood-group-system.jpg" alt="ABO blood group system" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fadi · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BlutAgglutination.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Karl Landsteiner was working at the Pathological-Anatomical Institute of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-vienna">University of Vienna</a> when he noticed in 1900 that red blood cells clumped together, or agglutinated, when mixed in test tubes with sera from different persons. He recorded it in a two-sentence footnote, which left open whether it reflected inborn differences between individuals or some damage of bacterial kind.</p><p>It was the first evidence that human blood varies, since all humans had been believed to have similar blood. In 1910 Ludwik Hirszfeld and Emil Freiherr von Dungern introduced the term 0 (null) for his group C and AB for the fourth type, and were the first to explain how the groups are inherited.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The system is controlled by a single gene, the ABO gene, on the long arm of the ninth chromosome. Its three classical alleles are IA, IB and i, and the gene encodes a glycosyltransferase, an enzyme that modifies the carbohydrate content of the red blood cell antigens. The IA allele gives type A, IB gives type B and i gives type O.</p><p>Because A and B are codominant, a type A and a type B parent can have an AB child. Felix Bernstein demonstrated the pattern of multiple alleles at one locus in 1924. The A type has about 20 subgroups, of which A1 makes up about 80 per cent of all A-type blood. Rare variants include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> phenotype and cis-AB.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Naming caused trouble for years. In 1907 the Czech serologist Jan Janský independently classified blood with the Roman numerals I to IV, while the American physician William L. Moss used the same numerals for a different order. Moss's system was adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the US, and Janský's in most European countries, which created confusion and danger in medical practice. A joint recommendation of 1921 favoured Janský's but was not followed where Moss's had been used.</p><p>In 1927 Landsteiner, by then at the Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, proposed the letters O, A, B and AB, and by the early 1950s they were universal. The American physician Reuben Ottenberg first used blood typing in transfusion in 1907, and large-scale use began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, once citric acid was used to prevent clotting.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ABO blood group system” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
